// async_engine/engine.d.rut — the async host set's engine rows
// The futures themselves never cross the boundary raw (the
// survey's law: engine-minted, compiler-owned); these rows cross the
// erasure box — `opaque(f)` seals a frame, `downcast<T>`
// recovers it — so the typed `Future<T> -> LaunchedFutureHandle<T>`
// arrow lives in rut code (the `async_host` inline package), where the
// type system can hold it.
//
// The crossings, one by one:
//   `__launch` — the launcher seals the frame from rut (`opaque(f)`);
//     the body unboxes to the frame slot and hands it to the driving
//     loop (the queue takes its own reference).
//   `__abort` — same seal; the body unboxes and flags + re-enqueues.
//   `__sleep` — the one engine-assisted mint: the body builds the sleep
//     frame and answers it SEALED under the `Future<nil>` object
//     spelling, which is what `sleep`'s `downcast<Future<nil>>`
//     recovers.
//   `__sleep_yield` — the sleep future's `Future::yield`: a bodyless
//     thunk the compiler mints whose `host_id` binds HERE. The yield
//     slot's ABI is engine-wide (every weaved async fn shares it: the
//     raw frame and cx), so the compiler mints a sealing wrapper that
//     boxes both registers before the row is reached — the wire at this
//     row is two boxes, and the body unboxes back to the raw pair.
//
// The engine-minted types themselves (`#frame@sleep`, the cx record)
// are unspellable in rut source — `opaque` is the honest crossing
// spelling: a sealed cell the body recovers through the erasure box.

pub host fn __launch(f: opaque);

pub host fn __abort(f: opaque) -> bool;

pub host fn __sleep(ms: u32) -> opaque;

pub host fn __sleep_yield(f: opaque, cx: opaque);
